    "mythology": {
      "body": "The ​stories of ‌**Yùhuáng** ‌circulate within ​the ​Chinese corpus, ​where ​the ​figure embodies ‌jade ​emperor, heaven, ​sovereignty.[^1]\n\n‌### Primary ​Attestation ‌(Sources)\n\nYùhuáng ​is ‌attested in ​the ​canonical Chinese ​sources ​as a ​figure ‌associated ​with jade ‌emperor, ​heaven, sovereignty.[^1]\n\n‌### ​Name and ‌Meaning ​(Etymology)\n\nThe ‌name ​means \"Jade ​Emperor\" ‌and reflects ‌the ‌figure's role ​in ​the ‌tradition. **Yùhuáng**, ​the ​Jade Emperor, ‌is ​the supreme ​ruler ‌of the ​Daoist ‌celestial bureaucracy. ​He ​presides over ​the ​Three Realms—heaven, ‌earth, ‌and ​the underworld—and ‌judges ​the dead ​at ‌the Chinese ‌New ‌Year.[^1] His ‌authority ​is administrative ‌rather ​than creative; ​he ​delegates cosmic ‌functions ​to ​a vast ​pantheon ‌of ministers ​and ​generals. His ​birthday, ‌celebrated on ​the ‌ninth day ​of ‌the first ​lunar ‌month, involves ‌offerings ​of ​incense, paper ​money, ​and vegetarian ‌food ‌across Chinese ​communities.[^1]",
